WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
After the Second World War,

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.780
the Netherlands had to
build a lot of new houses.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.220
Not just to repair damage,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.100
but to handle the population
from the post-war baby boom.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.840
And the housing they built was...

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.880
well, it was pretty much
all the same.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.940
Most of it built quickly, cheaply,
and to one of a few similar designs.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.460
So in 1968, the Dutch government's

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.860
Ministry for Public Housing
and Spatial Planning

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.520
started to offer grants
for experimental architecture.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.951
Because, to quote the
government minister in charge,

00:00:25.951 --> 00:00:30.060
"Men weet niet wat men mist".
You don't know what you're missing.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.420
Maybe they'd find a way to radically
improve housing for everyone.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.840
A lot of the experiments were very tame.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.360
You wouldn't really notice them
as you walked through a city,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.040
they just used different materials,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.620
or organised the rooms
or houses differently.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.100
And they actually did find
ways to build better homes.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.040
Some of the experiments
were the sort of

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.320
stark, brutalist work that
you might expect from

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.180
1960s or 70s architects.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.360
Interesting to look at, not always
great places to live.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.620
Some of those became quite famous,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.200
like the cube houses
in Helmond and Rotterdam.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.440
One of the final experiments
to get the subsidy

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.760
was built in 1984.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.160
And it was very strange indeed.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.080
The Bolwoningen,
the sphere houses,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.360
sit in Den Bosch in the
south of the country.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.700
According to the designer,
artist Dries Kreijkamp,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.580
humans were not meant
to live in a box.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.280
- It's not very spacious,
but depending on the person,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.860
like, I personally don't
have claustrophobia.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.840
But I have heard
from other people

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.420
that they have experiences
of claustrophobia when

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.420
they enter the house.
So, it's not made for everyone.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.720
- Kreijkamp was more a
designer than an architect.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.660
The experimental houses are
liveable, and they're still used as

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.940
social housing, where the
local government is the landlord.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.670
But there are definitely some problems.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.440
- Because of the trees,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.720
the shell of the house
gets green pretty quickly.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.680
Once in 5 or 10 years, they do
a complete cleaning of the houses.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.380
But anything inside the house,
that's all for yourself.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.840
Except for proper maintenance,

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.000
like the boilers and
that kind of stuff.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.440
Or if the windows are leaking,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.840
'cause that's one of the
biggest problems we have.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.780
On a good rainy day, you can basically
swim in your living quarters.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.240
The first floor is where
you enter the house.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.760
The second floor is
the sleeping quarters.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.260
Then you go another stair,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.660
and then you get to the
bathroom and the shower.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.600
And then you have the upper floor,
which is the living room, the kitchen.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.660
If you have a lot of stuff,
you have to adapt because

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.900
there's very limited room.
So you have to use every inch.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.500
Even if you measure, it's still
no guarantee that things will fit.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.060
The two halves of each house

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.100
were built in a temporary factory
on site here

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.180
and then connected together.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.240
These are made from glass fibre
reinforced concrete,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.880
with 10 centimetres of rockwool
insulation between

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.030
the outer and inner hull.
Which means they are warm.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.680
- If it is 20 degrees outside,
then it becomes 25 inside.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.180
So we cover the windows.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.980
We're always looking forward
to the winter, really.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.200
During winter it's quite
nice and comfy actually.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:50.820
I can't complain.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.620
- Maybe, if these plans continued,

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.020
the third or fourth generation
of these houses

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:55.860
would have worked well.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.700
But not only did they have problems,
they also proved just too expensive

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.160
compared to the economies of scale
for traditional housing.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.520
Recently these houses were
added monumental status,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.620
so they get a little bit more care.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.420
They can't be demolished.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.720
But that's only on the local level.
Not governmental level.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.040
At least not yet.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.100
- The surprising thing for me
is not that these houses exist.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.480
There's a lot of
weird houses out there.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.560
I've visited a few of them.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.760
What surprised me is:

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.740
they're still homes,
they're still lived in,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.380
they've not been converted
to hotels or short term lets

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:26.640
or quirky tourist attractions.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.860
And they're not sitting
disused and broken.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.800
The dreams of the space age
didn't all come to pass,

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.240
we don't all live in spheres now

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.160
and it turns out that there
are good reasons for that.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.080
But some people still do.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.340
- It's nice to live here,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.500
it's peaceful,
the shops are nearby,

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.060
you've got a park
next to your house,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.560
you can ice-skate in the winter.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.680
We have good neighbours,
we do hang out,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.760
we do chat with one another.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.260
You get used to it.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.560
It just asks a lot of adapting
to it. And being creative.

